 "text": "## Revenue is Heavily Enterprise-Concentrated\n\n**Total ARR is $21.2M across 372 customers** \u2014 with Enterprise driving 69% of that despite representing only 32% of the customer base.\n\n---\n\n### ARR Breakdown by Segment\n\n| Segment | Customers | ARR | % of ARR | Avg Deal Size | Bookings (Won) |\n|:---|---:|:---|:---|:---|:---|\n| Enterprise | 119 | $14.6M | 69% | $122.8K | $2.3M |\n| Mid-Market | 161 | $5.8M | 27% | $35.9K | $0.9M |\n| SMB | 92 | $0.8M | 4% | $9.0K | $9.2K |\n| **Total** | **372** | **$21.2M** | **100%** | **$57.1K** | **$3.2M** |\n\n---\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Enterprise is the growth engine.** With a $122.8K average deal size and $2.3M in bookings, Enterprise deals are ~3.4x more valuable than Mid-Market and ~14x more than SMB. Protecting and growing this segment should be the top priority.\n\n2. **Mid-Market is under-indexed.** Mid-Market has the *most customers* (161) but only 27% of ARR \u2014 suggesting significant expansion opportunity if those accounts can be upsold.\n\n3. **SMB is a minimal contributor.** At just 4% of ARR, SMB generates very little revenue relative to the customer support cost. Consider whether the PLG motion can handle SMB self-serve without heavy sales investment.\n\n### Recommendation\n**Double down on Enterprise acquisition and Mid-Market expansion.** Enterprise deals move the ARR needle fastest. Mid-Market represents an untapped pool of 161 existing customers with room to grow \u2014 a targeted expansion campaign could meaningfully shift the mix.",
